Asking which thread this is takes no lock

MessageManager::isThisTheMessageThread() took a std::mutex to compare the calling thread with the one the messages belong to. The question is asked on the audio thread: the VST3 wrapper asks it in JuceVST3EditController::paramChanged() to decide whether it may tell the host of a parameter change straight away or has to leave the value for the next block, and a parameter change is told from the audio callback -- which the documentation of AudioProcessorParameter::Listener says plainly, that many processors change their parameters during their audio callback and a handler must avoid blocking. The thread that is setting the message thread holds that mutex, so the audio thread waits for it.

The id is a std::atomic<Thread::ThreadID> now, as threadWithLock beside it already was, and the asking reads it without a lock. The mutex stays in the one that sets it, where it keeps the platform's part of that work -- the message window on Windows -- apart when two threads do it at once. The noexcept on the asking is honest for the first time: locking a std::mutex can throw, and that function was declared not to.

Two other readers were already reading the id without the mutex, currentThreadHasLockedMessageManager() and getCurrentMessageThread(), so what they were racing against is gone with the same change.

What found it is the realtime sanitizer, in ADLplug-Next's render test, which loads the plugin as a VST3 and sends it a program change: the plugin sets every parameter of a part from the audio callback, the wrapper is told of each, and it asks this question of each.

This is ADLplug-Next's own patch, applied to the submodule by cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures, and it goes away once the change is upstream.

